<LessonLayout title="4.7 — Decision Guide: Prompt Chain, Workflow or ReAct Agent?">

  <LessonHeader
    num="4.7"
    title="Decision Guide: Prompt Chain, Workflow or ReAct Agent?"
    subtitle="The same insurance claims built three ways, compared side by side, and a guide for choosing the design."
  />

  <div class="lesson-content">

    <LessonToc />

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">One Job, Three Designs <SourceBadge source="mine" /></h2>

    <p>Lessons 4.4 to 4.6 triaged the same car-insurance claims three ways:</p>

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Lesson</th><th>Design</th><th>In one line</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><a href={L('0009-exercise-claim-triage')}>4.4</a></td><td><strong>Prompt chain</strong></td><td>Fixed steps, every step a model call: extract → coverage → assess → route</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href={L('0016-exercise-claims-workflow')}>4.6</a></td><td><strong>Workflow</strong></td><td>Fixed steps, one LLM call to read the claim, the rest in code</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href={L('0013-exercise-claim-agent')}>4.5</a></td><td><strong>ReAct agent</strong></td><td>The model picks the next tool each turn; errors go back to it</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      The rest of this page puts them next to each other: how each one runs, what actually tells them apart, the
      same claim through all three, and the shape of the code. The columns are always in the same order: chain,
      workflow, ReAct agent, from most fixed to most flexible.
    </p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">A Fair Comparison: The Same Solution Space <SourceBadge source="mine" /></h2>

    <p>
      Comparing designs only means something if each one gets the <strong>same information</strong> and has to produce the
      <strong>same output</strong>. Otherwise you're comparing what each one was given, not how it's built. So on this page
      (and in the notebook below) all three get:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>the same claims, read by the same extraction step (incident type, damaged areas);</li>
      <li>the same policy table, price table and routing rules;</li>
      <li>the same queues to choose from, including <code>needs_review</code> for claims that aren't covered.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      What differs is <em>where the tables live and who uses them</em>. The chain has no tools, so the tables go into its
      prompts as text and the model does the lookup, the sum and the rule itself. The workflow's code reads the tables.
      The ReAct agent asks for tools, and the tools read the tables.
    </p>


    <h2 class="lesson-heading">How Each One Runs <SourceBadge source="mine" /></h2>

    <LessonDiagram definition={`
flowchart LR
  subgraph CH["🔗 Prompt chain"]
    direction TB
    c0["📄 claim"]:::in --> c1["🤖 extract"]:::llm --> c1g{{"🚦"}}:::gate --> c2["🤖 coverage<br/>policy table in prompt"]:::llm --> c2g{{"🚦"}}:::gate --> c3["🤖 assess<br/>price table in prompt"]:::llm --> c3g{{"🚦"}}:::gate --> c5["🤖 route<br/>rules in prompt"]:::llm --> c5g{{"🚦"}}:::gate --> c4["📥 queue"]:::done
    c1g -.->|"retry"| c1
    c3g -.->|"retry"| c3
  end
  subgraph WF["⚙️ Workflow"]
    direction TB
    w0["📄 claim"]:::in --> w1["🤖 extract"]:::llm --> w1g{{"🚦"}}:::gate --> w2["🛠️ check_coverage"]:::code --> w3["🛠️ price_damage"]:::code --> w4["🧮 severity"]:::code --> w5["🛠️ route_claim"]:::code --> w6["📥 queue"]:::done
    w1g -.->|"retry"| w1
    w2 -.->|"not covered"| w7["👤 review"]:::warn
  end
  subgraph AG["🤖 ReAct agent"]
    direction TB
    a0["📄 claim"]:::in --> a1["🤖 THINK + ACT<br/>picks a tool"]:::llm --> a2["🛠️ the tool it picked"]:::code
    a2 -.->|"OBSERVE"| a1
    a1 -->|"final_answer"| a3{{"🚦 final gate"}}:::gate --> a4["📥 queue"]:::done
    a3 -.->|"ERROR"| a1
  end
  CH ~~~ WF ~~~ AG

  classDef in   fill:#f1f5f9,color:#0f172a,stroke:#64748b,stroke-width:2px
  classDef llm  fill:#dbeafe,color:#1e3a8a,stroke:#2563eb,stroke-width:2px
  classDef code fill:#fef3c7,color:#78350f,stroke:#d97706,stroke-width:2px
  classDef gate fill:#ede9fe,color:#4c1d95,stroke:#7c3aed,stroke-width:2px
