      <li><strong>Compare all three on every claim.</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Your turn:</strong> the chain without retries (<code>MAX_ATTEMPTS = 1</code>), the ReAct agent's path across runs, the chain with a million policies, a claim of your own.</li>
    </ol>

    <NotebookView notebook={compareNb} path="code/course-1/4.7-compare-designs/compare_designs.ipynb" />

    <LessonCallout variant="tip" title="Running it in Colab">
      <p>
        <strong>Run the cells top to bottom, setup first.</strong> The notebook uses Colab's built-in models, no API key
        needed. Start with C004 in all three designs with <code>VERBOSE = True</code>: the chain shows four model
        answers, the workflow one answer and four code steps, the ReAct agent every THINK / ACT / OBSERVE turn.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Trade-offs <SourceBadge source="mine" /></h2>

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th></th><th>Prompt chain</th><th>Workflow</th><th>ReAct agent</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Who picks the next step</td><td>Code</td><td>Code</td><td>The model</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Where the LLM is used</td><td>Every step</td><td>Only where text needs reading or writing</td><td>Every turn, to choose and to read</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Same input, same path?</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Not always</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cost and latency</td><td>Medium: one call per step</td><td>Lowest</td><td>Highest, and it varies</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Testing</td><td>Test each step</td><td>Unit-test the code; test the LLM steps</td><td>Test tools and gates; evaluate whole traces</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Debugging</td><td>Find the failing step</td><td>Find the failing step</td><td>Read the THINK/ACT/OBSERVE trace</td></tr>
          <tr><td>A new kind of case</td><td>New step or prompt change</td><td>New branch in code</td><td>Often a new tool or a line in a description</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Best when</td><td>Every step needs language, in a known order</td><td>Steps are known; some are rules or lookups</td><td>The path depends on what you find</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Cost of Running <SourceBadge source="mine" /></h2>

    <p>
      What each claim costs, every time it runs. Token counts are rough (about 4 characters per token), measured from the
      notebook's prompts for claim C004 with no retries.
    </p>

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Per claim</th><th>Prompt chain</th><th>Workflow</th><th>ReAct agent</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Model calls</td><td>4, always</td><td>1</td><td>4–5, more after mistakes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Input tokens</td><td>≈ 670</td><td>≈ 180</td><td>≈ 2,300</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Why</td><td>Four prompts, two of them carrying a table</td><td>One short prompt and the claim</td><td>The whole history is resent every turn, and it grows each turn</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Latency</td><td>4 round trips, one after another</td><td>1 round trip, then fast code</td><td>4–5 round trips; varies per claim</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Predictable cost?</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>No: depends on the path the model takes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>As the business grows</td><td>Prompts grow with the data: about 15 tokens per policy in the coverage prompt. A million policies don't fit.</td><td>Flat: code queries the data</td><td>Flat per turn: tools query the data</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      Roughly 1 : 4 : 12 in tokens for the same answer. At ten claims a day nobody notices; at a hundred thousand a day it
      decides the budget, and the latency decides whether a customer waits for an answer or gets an email later. Retries
      add to all three, but they hit the ReAct agent hardest: every extra turn resends the longest history.
    </p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Cost of Change <SourceBadge source="mine" /></h2>

    <p>
      What it takes to change the system later. This is where the designs differ most, and it's easy to forget when
      choosing.
    </p>

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Change</th><th>Prompt chain</th><th>Workflow</th><th>ReAct agent</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>A repair price changes</td><td>Edit the table text in a prompt, re-test the prompt</td><td>Edit the data; nothing else</td><td>Edit the data; nothing else</td></tr>
          <tr><td>A new coverage rule</td><td>Rewrite the coverage prompt and check the model applies it</td><td>Change <code>check_coverage</code>, add a unit test</td><td>Change the tool; maybe its description</td></tr>
          <tr><td>A new queue (e.g. injuries go to a <code>bodily_injury</code> team)</td><td>Route prompt and its output model</td><td>One branch in code, one extracted field</td><td>A line in the prompt and the final gate</td></tr>
          <tr><td>A new kind of claim with a different path (a theft: no pricing, a police report first)</td><td>Hard: a chain is a straight line, so every step's prompt has to handle it</td><td>A new branch, a new field, new tests; fine once, painful the tenth time</td><td>Often a new tool and a line in its description; it finds the path</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Switch to a new model</td><td>Re-check 4 prompts</td><td>Re-check 1 prompt</td><td>Re-check whole traces: the paths themselves can change</td></tr>
          <tr><td>A million policies</td><td>Redesign: move the lookup into code (it becomes a workflow)</td><td>Swap the dict for a database query</td><td>Swap the dict for a database query</td></tr>
          <tr><td>An auditor asks why a claim got its queue</td><td>Four logged answers</td><td>The same code path every time; easiest</td><td>Read the trace; the path differs per claim</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      The pattern: the <strong>workflow</strong> is cheapest to change for anything you can predict (prices, rules, queues),
      because the logic is plain code with tests. The <strong>ReAct agent</strong> is cheapest for what you can't predict (new kinds
      of cases), because it finds the path itself, but every change needs whole-trace evaluation instead of unit tests.
      The <strong>chain</strong> keeps logic and data in prompt text, which is the hardest place to change, test and scale.
    </p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Testability <SourceBadge source="mine" /></h2>

    <p>
      Two kinds of test apply here. A <strong>unit test</strong> checks plain code: no model call, free to run, the same
      result every time, and a failure points to a line. An <strong>evaluation</strong> checks a model step: it costs
      model calls, the result can vary, so you run a labeled set several times and measure how often it's right. The more of
      the logic lives in code, the more of the system unit tests can cover.
    </p>

    <LessonDiagram definition={`
flowchart LR
  subgraph CH["🔗 Prompt chain"]
    direction TB
    c1["🎲 extract"]:::ev --> c1g{{"✅ gate"}}:::ut --> c2["🎲 coverage"]:::ev --> c2g{{"✅ gate"}}:::ut --> c3["🎲 assess"]:::ev --> c3g{{"✅ gate"}}:::ut --> c4["🎲 route"]:::ev --> c4g{{"✅ gate"}}:::ut
  end
