    <p>
      Green ✅ boxes can be unit-tested; orange 🎲 boxes need evaluations. The chain is mostly orange: only its gates are
      plain code. The workflow is mostly green: one orange box at the top. The ReAct agent's tools, parser and final gate are
      green, but the most important part, the decisions it makes every turn, is orange.
    </p>

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th></th><th>Prompt chain</th><th>Workflow</th><th>ReAct agent</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Unit-testable (no model)</td><td>Only the gates</td><td>Everything after extraction: coverage, price, severity, route, every branch, the gate</td><td>Tools, parser, executor, final gate (the notebooks' "you play the model" cells)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Needs evaluations</td><td>All four steps: lookup, sum, rule, route</td><td>Only the extraction</td><td>The decisions: which tool, which arguments, in what order, when to stop</td></tr>
          <tr><td>What an evaluation compares</td><td>Each step's output with the expected one</td><td>Extracted facts with labeled facts</td><td>Whole runs: the final answer, and whether the path was sensible</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Same test, same result?</td><td>Steps are fixed; outputs vary</td><td>Path fixed; only extraction varies</td><td>Path and outputs vary: run each case several times</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cost of a full test run</td><td>4 model calls per case</td><td>1 call per case; unit tests free</td><td>4–5 calls per case, times the repeats</td></tr>
          <tr><td>When a test fails</td><td>The failing step's output shows it</td><td>A stack trace, or the extraction's output</td><td>Read the trace; the mistake may be several turns before the wrong answer</td></tr>
          <tr><td>After a model upgrade</td><td>Re-run 4 step evaluations</td><td>Re-run 1 extraction evaluation</td><td>Re-run every scenario, several times each</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>What each kind of test looks like, using the notebooks' functions:</p>

    <CodeBlock lang="python" label="unit test · gate test · evaluation" code={testShape} />

    <p>
      Testability follows the same line as everything else on this page: whatever moves from a prompt into code becomes
      cheap, fast and exact to test. That's one more reason to keep rules and numbers in code whichever design you choose,
      and to reach for a ReAct agent only where its flexibility is worth evaluations instead of unit tests.
    </p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">The Terms <SourceBadge source="mine" /></h2>

    <ul>
      <li>
        A <strong>prompt chain</strong> is a workflow whose steps are all LLM calls, each one's output feeding the next
        (lesson 4.2).
      </li>
      <li>
        A <strong>workflow</strong> is a fixed set of steps that your code runs, with branches. Some steps call an LLM,
        some call tools or plain functions. Lesson 4.2's "hard-coded workflow" (the dental appointment) is one.
      </li>
      <li>
        A <strong>ReAct agent</strong> is an agent in the course's sense (lesson 4.1): the LLM reasons, plans and decides
        when to use tools, in a THINK → ACT → OBSERVE loop, until it gives a final answer or hits a limit (lessons 2.1 and 4.5).
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Which of them is an agent?</h3>

    <p>
      The course's definition (lesson <a href={L('0006-ai-agents-building-blocks')}>4.1</a>): <em>an AI agent is an
      AI-driven application that uses an LLM as its brain to reason, plan, and autonomously take actions in an
      environment, often using external tools.</em> Its orchestration layer <em>lets the LLM decide when to use tools</em>.
      Applied to the three designs:
    </p>

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th></th><th>Prompt chain</th><th>Workflow</th><th>ReAct agent</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>LLM reasons</td><td>Yes, inside each step</td><td>Yes, inside its one step</td><td>Yes, every turn</td></tr>
          <tr><td>LLM plans the steps</td><td>No: your code fixed them</td><td>No: your code fixed them</td><td><strong>Yes</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>LLM decides when to use tools</td><td>No tools</td><td>No: your code calls them</td><td><strong>Yes</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Takes actions autonomously</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td><strong>Yes</strong>, within the final gate</td></tr>
          <tr><td>An agent, by the course's definition?</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td><strong>Yes</strong></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      So only the ReAct design is an agent. The chain and the workflow are what lesson 4.2 calls <em>agentic
      workflows</em>: the building blocks agents are made from, and often the better choice on their own. That's why the
      title says <em>ReAct agent</em>, and why lesson 4.2's comparison is "hard-coded workflow vs. AI agent (ReAct)".
    </p>

    <p>They sit on a scale, from most predictable to most flexible:</p>

    <LessonDiagram definition={`
flowchart LR
  A["📝 Single prompt"]:::p --> B["🔗 Prompt chain"]:::p --> C["⚙️ Workflow<br/>code + LLM steps"]:::w --> D["⚙️🤖 Workflow with<br/>one ReAct agent step"]:::w --> E["🤖 ReAct agent"]:::a
  classDef p fill:#dcfce7,color:#14532d,stroke:#16a34a,stroke-width:2px
  classDef w fill:#fef3c7,color:#78350f,stroke:#d97706,stroke-width:2px
  classDef a fill:#dbeafe,color:#1e3a8a,stroke:#2563eb,stroke-width:2px
    `} />

    <p>
      Moving right buys flexibility and costs predictability, money and latency. The guide below is about moving
      only as far right as the problem needs.
    </p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">When to Use What <SourceBadge source="mine" /></h2>

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th></th><th>Prompt chain</th><th>Workflow</th><th>ReAct agent</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Use it when</strong></td><td>Every step is a language task (summarize → translate → reformat), the steps are fixed, and there's little data or arithmetic</td><td>The steps are known, some are rules, lookups or maths, and volume, latency or audits matter</td><td>The next step depends on what the last one found, cases vary a lot, or new kinds of case keep appearing</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Avoid it when</strong></td><td>Steps need data lookups or exact numbers, or the data is large</td><td>Paths keep multiplying into special cases</td><td>The path is the same for every input, or every call's cost and latency matter</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Running cost</td><td>Medium, fixed</td><td>Lowest, fixed</td><td>Highest, variable</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Testability</td><td>Low: only the gates are unit-testable</td><td>High: everything but extraction is unit-testable</td><td>Tools and gate unit-testable; decisions need repeated evaluations</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cost of change</td><td>High: logic and data live in prompt text</td><td>Low for expected changes, high for new kinds of case</td><td>Low for new kinds of case, but every change needs whole-trace evaluation</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Examples</td><td>Draft → critique → rewrite a marketing post; transcript → summary → action items</td><td>Claim triage, invoice matching, structured form intake, claim-button requests (4.8)</td><td>Research questions, open-ended support emails (4.8), debugging with tools</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      Mixing is normal: a workflow with one ReAct agent step where the path varies, or a ReAct agent whose tools are small
      workflows. The decision guide below is for choosing each part.
    </p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">The Decision Guide <SourceBadge source="mine" /></h2>

    <LessonDiagram definition={`
flowchart TB
  S["🧩 A task"]:::in --> Q1{{"Can one well-written prompt<br/>do it reliably?"}}:::q
